The note concludes from Propositions 5.3–5.5 that information systems and approximable mappings form a category, a subcategory of the category of sets, and then Proposition 5.6: for an element \(b\) of \(\mathbf{B}\) there is a unique approximable mapping \(\mathrm{const}\,b:\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\) with value \(b\) at every element of \(\mathbf{A}\); \(f\circ(\mathrm{const}\,b)=\mathrm{const}\,f(b)\) for \(f:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\); and \((\mathrm{const}\,b)\circ g=\mathrm{const}\,b\) for \(g:\mathbf{D}\to\mathbf{A}\). `constMap` defines the relation to hold when the codomain set is contained in \(b\), notes that this codomain set is consistent because \(b\) is an element, and checks the empty relation, closure of subsets of \(b\) under union, and monotonicity from deductive closure of \(b\). `constMap.toElement` proves the constant value: a token of the image lies in a finite subset of \(b\), and a token of \(b\) is reached because the empty set is a consistent subset of the argument and the singleton of that token is contained in \(b\). `comp_constMap` is clause (ii) and `constMap.comp` is clause (iii); each reduces equality of mappings to equality of images by extensionality and rewrites the images by the action of composition and the constant value. `constMap.unique` is the uniqueness clause, by the same extensionality. `Proposition56` conjoins (i)–(iii) as `theorem_56`. `Basic` includes uniqueness as well as the two absorption laws. The constant-map clauses match the proposition. The unit and associativity laws required for the category assertion in the lead-in are not proved. **Theorem:** partial — (i)–(iii) and uniqueness are established, while the category laws are not. **Proof:** partial — the relation axioms, the constant-value inclusions, and the two absorption identities are proved, and there is no calculation of identity or associativity laws.
